            "parents": [
                "ca994a78654bf7122510c1aafd970bf5a7e89f4d"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/92/83992/4",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "rcheng123",
                "email": "rocheng@nvidia.com",
                "username": "rcheng123"
            },
            "createdOn": 1758739503,
            "author": {
                "name": "rcheng123",
                "email": "rocheng@nvidia.com",
                "username": "rcheng123"
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Please attempt to answer \"why\" in your commit message.  We can see the \"what\" from the code, but it doesn't really explain why certain choices were made."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rcheng123",
                        "email": "rocheng@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "rcheng123"
                    },
                    "message": "updated. for testing/dev purpose we need a streamlined way to write to fru from .bin file. yes we already have writeFru and perhaps we can have a linux command or bash script that cat .bin file and pipe to writeFRU. But i think my proposal is the clean way to go."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 9,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> streamlined.... clean\n\nI'm really not sure what this means.  Can we talk about (in the commit message) why this is better than just a shell command?\n\n\"streamlined\" and \"clean\" are very abstract.  Maybe this is about being able to check the fru before write?  Maybe this is not having a documented understanding of the existing i2c CLI tools?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rcheng123",
                        "email": "rocheng@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "rcheng123"
                    },
                    "message": "This is a proposal to write to fru from a .bin file. Idea is the same as existing dbus method WriteFru, but we are writing from .bin file instead of passing the arguments in dbus command. We need this as many of our fru data are stored in .bin file and need a good way to write fru from file."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Why would we use this over just cat file > /sys/..../eeprom ?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Trying to understand the use case for this if this is a development tool.\n\nIf this is intended to be used elsewhere in the system, I suspect we need some more security in the API against writing bad content or devices that aren't eeproms."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rcheng123",
                        "email": "rocheng@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "rcheng123"
                    },
                    "message": "how is this different from directly calling dbus method writeFru? writeFRU has all the sanity checks."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "WriteFru isn't used by anything externally, that's the point."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "offsets, and flexibility in implementation.\n\nFor example:\n\n- one could add protections that the data we are using to overwrite actually parses as valid fru data.\n\n- preventing overwrite of serial number if that's already been written.\n\n- making sure the length of data actually fits before writing\n\nThere is a lot of benefits to having a high level API here."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> WriteFru isn't used by anything externally, that's the point.\n\nWrong. There is 4 usages\n\nhttps://github.com/search?q=org%3Aopenbmc+%2F%22WriteFru%22%2F&type=code\n\nand one of which is in ipmid, which is still supported and used by quite a bunch of folks.\n\nhttps://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-host-ipmid/blob/6b47d5a1da42dca58eff80c6f348eb4e719b427b/dbus-sdr/storagecommands.cpp#L121\n\nIf i remember right you can even trigger this API via `ipmitool` over the network."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/fru_device/fru_device.cpp",
                    "line": 60,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This isn't really an IPC mechanism to read from a fixed path.  If we want to send a file, we should be using an fd as the argument."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/fru_device/fru_device.cpp",
                    "line": 60,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "rcheng123",
                        "email": "rocheng@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "rcheng123"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/fru_device/fru_device.cpp",
                    "line": 60,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Current code now passes a file path, not a FD."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/fru_device/fru_device.cpp",
                    "line": 1048,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
